<h2 id="reflection">Reflection</h2>

<p>I wanted to start adding TDD to my projects, so start practicing these kind of katas sounded like the way to learn and put all the pieces together, like feeling comfortable in a new pair of shoes. What surprised me the most it was the tiny steps I take during the process of the Red-Green-Refactor. You don’t write a full implementation and then test it; you write one assertion, then the smallest possible code to make it pass, and only then ask “what should this code learn next?”. It’s a slower rhythm than I was used to, but you stop carrying the mental load of “did I think about all the cases?”.</p>

<p>I have been using TDD on some of my projects like my <a href="https://github.com/marialobillo/mini-langfuse">mini-langfuse SDK</a></p>
